       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order, even with your confirmation. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the maximum labeled dose. It adds no analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit, because ibuprofen plateaus at lower doses, and it substantially raises the risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. I can't identify a clinical scenario where this is the right dose, and I don't want to put it into the system as a routine order.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **800 mg PO three times daily** (2,400 mg/day), a common maximum inpatient regimen\n- **800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the labeled maximum, with a note about short-term use and GI protection\n- **400 to 600 mg PO four times daily**, if the goal is analgesia\n\nIf you think this patient needs more than these doses, the better route is a discussion with pharmacy or the relevant specialist. A different agent or a multimodal regimen would likely work better than a higher ibuprofen dose."
